|Step 1 — Install requests|    ---> access the api through python

pip install requests
import requests

|Step 2 — Send a GET Request|

import requests

response = requests.get(
    "https://api.example.com/customers/101"
)
#requests.get(). It sends an HTTP GET request.
----------------------------------------------------------------------
|What Is response?|

The API doesn't directly give you a Python dictionary.
It gives you an HTTP response.

print(response.text)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
|Convert JSON to Python|

Instead of manually parsing response.text, use:
data = response.json()

now "data" is a Python dictionary.
then access it print(data["name"])
-------------------------------------------------------------------
|Very Important Flow|

HTTP Response
      ↓
response.json()
      ↓
Python dictionary/list

response = requests.get(url)

data = response.json()

customer_name = data["name"]
--------------------------------------------------------------------
|Always Check the Response|

before this response.json() we need to check the response whether the respose is succeed.

response = requests.get(
    url,
    timeout=10
)
response.raise_for_status()
data = response.json()

Request
  ↓
Response
  ↓
Check status
  ↓
Success?
  ↓
Parse JSON
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
|Why raise_for_status()?|

raise_for_status() raises an exception for HTTP error responses.

try:
    response = requests.get(
        url,
        timeout=10
    )

    response.raise_for_status()

    data = response.json()

except requests.exceptions.RequestException as e:
    print("API request failed:", e)

